                <pb facs="0584" n="572" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg572"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/11871: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Leopoldville</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Mallon</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Leopoldville</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-09T10:00:00+01:00">June 9, 1941—10
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 10—5:53 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to my telegram of June 6, 4 p.m. The High Commissioner of Free French
                    Africa has sent me a memorandum to the effect that the following with spare
                    parts and ammunition is urgently needed for the defense of Free French Africa: 6
                    transport or ambulance twin engine airplanes Douglas or Lockheed type; 10
                    bombing planes of the type Glenn Martin; 60 Pom-Pom guns or similar antiaircraft
                    guns of small caliber; 40 anti-tank guns of about 1-inch caliber.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mallon</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
